<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manitoba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manitoba is a province of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, with Winnipeg as its capital. It was created in 1870 from part of Rupert's Land after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-river-resistance">Red River Resistance</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a>, whose provisional government negotiated its entry into Confederation, and was enlarged northwards to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a> in 1912.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manitoba.jpg" alt="Manitoba" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kooma (original) Echando una mano (current) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Manitoba.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Macdonald, prime minister in 1867–1873 and 1878–1891, expanded the dominion by acquiring Rupert’s Land and the North-Western Territory from the Hudson’s Bay Company in 1870, creating <b>Manitoba</b>; British Columbia joined in 1871 on the promise of a railway, and Prince Edward Island in 1873.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…five Canadian provinces before 1896, which led to the idea that it was made at the time of the Wolseley Expedition of 1870 to the Red River Valley of <b>Manitoba</b>, as the lament of a local woman, perhaps a Métis, whose soldier lover was about to return east.</blockquote><a href="/songs/red-river-valley">Red River Valley</a></li><li><blockquote>Promised land for the Métis was largely lost, but Riel is now honoured in <b>Manitoba</b> as its founder.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-assiniboia">Provisional Government of Assiniboia</a></li><li><blockquote>Negotiations led to the creation of <b>Manitoba</b> as a province of Canada in 1870.</blockquote><a href="/state/red-river-colony">Red River Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>The grant covered much of what is now northern Quebec and Ontario, <b>Manitoba</b>, Saskatchewan, Alberta and parts of the northern United States, though the company knew little of the interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/ruperts-land">Rupert's Land</a></li></ul></section>
